# Attenuate — Build Spec

Implementation spec. No strategy, no judging notes.

**Stack:** Solidity 0.8.26 · Foundry · ENSv2 on Sepolia · TypeScript / Node 20 · Ledger Key Ring CLI (`wallet-cli ring`) · The Graph (Subgraph MCP, Substreams gRPC) · x402 · Next.js + viem

> **DEADLINE: Sunday 13 September 2026, 12:00 pm EDT (9:30 pm IST). Internal freeze Friday 12 Sep.**
>
> **This file must be committed to the submission repo.** ETHOnline requires that spec-driven workflows include all spec files, prompts and planning artifacts so judges can see how the AI was directed. Keep a `prompts/` directory alongside it and commit as you go.
>
> **Every team member needs real authored commits.** Submissions relying entirely on AI without meaningful team contribution may be ineligible for partner prizes.

---

## ⚠️ Read before writing any code

**1. Everything marked `[UNVERIFIED]` must be confirmed against live ENSv2 contracts on Sepolia before implementation.** ENSv2 is three months old. Do not invent function signatures. Pull real ABIs from Sepolia and from the `ens-cli` source (`github.com/ensdomains/ens-cli`). If a signature in this spec conflicts with the real ABI, the real ABI wins — update this file.

**2. Enforcement lives in contracts, not TypeScript.** The broker must never be the thing that decides whether a grant is legal. It is tempting and easier to write the subset check in `broker/grant.ts`. Do not. The check belongs in `AttenuatedSubregistry.register()`. A TypeScript-only check makes the entire project theatre.

**3. Build order is strict.** Do not scaffold all directories at once.

```
1. contracts/  — attenuation invariant + fuzz tests GREEN before anything else
2. ENS integration — real subregistry, real subname, real resolver write
3. Ledger — Key Ring root mandate
4. Graph — subgraph, MCP, Substreams, x402
5. web/ — one screen
```

