## 9. The Graph

### 9.1 Own subgraph (`subgraph/`)

Index the permission tree itself.

```graphql
type Agent @entity {
  id: ID!                    # namehash
  name: String!              # ens name
  parent: Agent
  children: [Agent!]! @derivedFrom(field: "parent")
  capabilities: BigInt!
  spendCap: BigInt!
  remaining: BigInt!
  queryBudget: BigInt!
  expiry: BigInt!
  depth: Int!
  epoch: BigInt!
  revoked: Boolean!
  createdAt: BigInt!
}

type Escalation @entity {
  id: ID!
  attemptedBy: Agent!
  reason: String!            # SCOPE_WIDENED, CAP_EXCEEDS_UNALLOCATED, ...
  blockNumber: BigInt!
  timestamp: BigInt!
}

type BudgetFlow @entity {
  id: ID!
  from: Agent!
  to: Agent
  amount: BigInt!
  kind: String!              # ALLOCATE | SPEND | RETURN
  timestamp: BigInt!
}
```

Handlers: `Granted`, `Revoked`, `Executed`, `EscalationBlocked`.

**Fallback if day 4 slips:** ship a minimal schema indexing only `Granted` and `Revoked`. An indexed subgraph beats a perfect unindexed one.

### 9.2 Substreams trigger (`indexer/`)

gRPC subscription with cursor resumption and reorg handling. **Not polling.**

- Persist the cursor; resume from it on restart
- On `BlockUndoSignal`, roll back agent state to the last irreversible block and emit a reorg event to the UI
- Rationale for the writeup: a guard five minutes late is a preference for an app that displays and a bug for one that spends

### 9.3 x402 metered budget

```
agent → gated Graph endpoint
     ← 402 Payment Required
agent → pay from Grant.queryBudget (debited onchain)
     ← data
```

The budget spent on data is the budget the device authorised. Same object. Debit `queryBudget` on the Grant, not a separate counter.

### 9.4 MCP server (`mcp-server/`)

Reusable infrastructure, not a wrapper around the demo. Any agent in any framework must be able to use it without seeing this project.

Tools to expose: `grant_capability`, `revoke_agent`, `check_scope`, `query_position`, `get_delegation_tree`.

Ship `SKILL.md` alongside.

### 9.5 Benchmark

Measure and publish:

| Metric | Method |
|---|---|
| Trigger lag: Substreams vs polling | 100 blocks, both running in parallel |
| Missed events | Liquidation-threshold crossings polling would have missed |
| Reorg recovery time | Inject a reorg on a fork, measure state correction |

---

## 10. Agent (`agent/`)

Loop:

```
1. Substreams push → position health changed
2. Read position via Subgraph MCP (pay x402 from queryBudget)
3. health > safe          → no action
   health approaching     → delegate: mint risk.attenuate.eth (read-only, metered)
   health critical        → delegate: mint exec.attenuate.eth (one cap, one target, minutes)
   beyond mandate         → escalate to device
4. Child executes via Executor, reports, parent revokes the child name
```

Sub-agents are separate processes with their own keys resolving to their own names. **They must not be function calls with names attached.** A judge will check this.

